    <!-- ===================================================
         THE BIG FLAW ANALYSIS
         =================================================== -->
    <hr class="p7-sep" />
    <div class="p7-section-label">Critical Engineering Challenge</div>
    <h2 class="p7-section-title">The fundamental flaw — and why Phase 7 comes closer than any prior phase</h2>

    <div class="p7-flaw-hero">
        <div class="p7-flaw-left">
            <div class="p7-flaw-tag-big">⚡ THERMODYNAMIC CONSTRAINT</div>
            <h3>You cannot heat more steam than your alternators can replace — but you can minimize the gap</h3>
            <p>
                Every prior phase used battery power to heat water into steam. The steam did work.
                But the work output through an alternator never returned enough electricity to fully power the heater —
                because Rankine cycle efficiency caps at ~18–22% for small systems, meaning 78–82% of heat input
                is lost to the condenser. You can't beat thermodynamics.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
                Phase 7 doesn't try to. Instead, it maximises the percentage returned.
                With 6 alternators at 150A × 12V = 1,800W each (10,800W total raw), and a transformer/rectifier
                efficiency of ~92%, regen return is ~9,936W. The heater at 70% duty draws ~3,500W.
                <strong style="color:#00ff88;">Phase 7 can return 2.8× the heater draw</strong> — the excess goes
                back to Battery A, extending range dramatically.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
                The PCW balances this dynamically: at low throttle the heater runs at 30% duty (~1,500W draw),
                regen still returns ~4,000W, net charging Battery A. At high throttle the heater runs at 100%
                (~5,000W), regen covers ~8,000W, still net positive. The gap only opens at maximum load
                when mechanical output is at peak.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p7-flaw-right">
            <div class="p7-flaw-math">
                <div class="p7-math-row">
                    <span class="p7-math-label">6 alternators × 150A × 12V</span>
                    <span class="p7-math-eq">= 10,800 W raw</span>
                </div>
                <div class="p7-math-row">
                    <span class="p7-math-label">× transformer η (97%)</span>
                    <span class="p7-math-eq">= 10,476 W</span>
                </div>
